    "## 2. Selection Sort - Simple Selection\n",
    "\n",
    "**What it is:**\n",
    "Divide the list into a sorted portion (left) and unsorted portion (right). Repeatedly find the smallest element in the unsorted part and move it to the sorted part.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How it works:**\n",
    "1. Start with position 0 (this will be sorted part)\n",
    "2. Find the minimum value in the rest of the array\n",
    "3. Swap the minimum with the current position\n",
    "4. Move to position 1 and repeat\n",
    "5. Continue until you reach the end\n",
    "\n",
    "**Example: Sorting [64, 25, 12, 22, 11]**\n",
    "```\n",
    "Find min (11) → Swap with 64: [11, 25, 12, 22, 64]\n",
    "Find min (12) → Swap with 25: [11, 12, 25, 22, 64]\n",
    "Find min (22) → Swap with 25: [11, 12, 22, 25, 64]\n",
    "Find min (25) → Already in place: [11, 12, 22, 25, 64] ✓\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "**Time Complexity:**\n",
    "- Best: O(n²)\n",
    "- Average: O(n²)\n",
    "- Worst: O(n²)\n",
    "- **Always O(n²)** regardless of input\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why it matters for LLMs:**\n",
    "- Predictable performance (always same time)\n",
    "- Minimal swaps (good when writes are expensive)\n",
    "- Used in embedded systems with limited memory\n",
    "\n",
    "**Requirements:**\n",
    "- Sort array in ascending order\n",
    "- Minimize number of swaps (key characteristic)\n",
    "- Handle empty arrays and single elements"
   ]
