    "## 4. QuickSort - Divide and Conquer (Average O(n log n))\n",
    "\n",
    "**What it is:**\n",
    "Pick a pivot element, partition the array around it (smaller on left, larger on right), then recursively sort each side.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How it works:**\n",
    "1. Choose a pivot (typically last element, but can be anything)\n",
    "2. Partition: Move all smaller elements left of pivot, larger elements right\n",
    "3. Recursively sort left partition\n",
    "4. Recursively sort right partition\n",
    "5. (No merge needed—in-place sorting)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Example: Sorting [5, 2, 8, 1, 9]**\n",
    "```\n",
    "Pivot 9: [5, 2, 8, 1] | 9 | []\n",
    "Left side pivot 1: [] | 1 | [5, 2, 8]\n",
    "Right side pivot 8: [5, 2] | 8 | []\n",
    "Continue recursively...\n",
    "Result: [1, 2, 5, 8, 9] ✓\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "**Time Complexity:**\n",
    "- Best: O(n log n)\n",
    "- Average: O(n log n) ← Most common!\n",
    "- Worst: O(n²) if pivot choices are bad\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why it matters for LLMs:**\n",
    "- Fast in practice (used in most standard libraries)\n",
    "- In-place (doesn't need extra memory)\n",
    "- Foundation for understanding divide-and-conquer\n",
    "- Can add heuristics (pick random pivot, median-of-three)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Requirements:**\n",
    "- Sort array in ascending order\n",
    "- Implement the partition logic\n",
    "- Handle empty arrays and single elements"
   ]
