    "## 5. MergeSort - Guaranteed O(n log n)\n",
    "\n",
    "**What it is:**\n",
    "Divide the array in half recursively until you have single elements, then merge them back together in sorted order.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How it works:**\n",
    "1. **Divide**: Split array in half recursively until size = 1\n",
    "2. **Conquer**: Merge two sorted halves:\n",
    "   - Compare first elements of each half\n",
    "   - Take the smaller one, add to result\n",
    "   - Move pointer forward in that half\n",
    "   - Repeat until both halves are exhausted\n",
    "\n",
    "**Example: Sorting [5, 2, 8, 1, 9]**\n",
    "```\n",
    "Divide: [5,2] [8,1,9]\n",
    "        [5] [2] [8] [1] [9]\n",
    "Merge:  [2,5]   [1,8,9]\n",
    "        [1,2,5,8,9] ✓\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "**Time Complexity:**\n",
    "- Best: O(n log n)\n",
    "- Average: O(n log n)\n",
    "- Worst: O(n log n) ← **GUARANTEED!**\n",
    "- Space: O(n) (needs extra array for merging)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why it matters for LLMs:**\n",
    "- **Predictable performance**: Always O(n log n)\n",
    "- **Stable**: Equal elements keep their relative order\n",
    "- Good for linked lists (no random access needed)\n",
    "- Used when consistency is more important than speed\n",
    "- Basis for understanding merge algorithms (used in databases)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Requirements:**\n",
    "- Sort array in ascending order\n",
    "- Implement merge step correctly\n",
    "- Handle empty arrays and single elements"
   ]
