    "## Sorting Algorithm Comparison Table\n",
    "\n",
    "| Algorithm | Avg Time | Best Case | Worst Case | Space | Stable | When to Use |\n",
    "|-----------|----------|-----------|------------|-------|--------|-------------|\n",
    "| Bubble Sort | O(n²) | O(n)* | O(n²) | O(1) | Yes | Learning only; never in production |\n",
    "| Selection Sort | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(1) | No | When writes are expensive |\n",
    "| Insertion Sort | O(n²) | O(n) | O(n²) | O(1) | Yes | Nearly sorted data, small arrays |\n",
    "| QuickSort | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n²) | O(log n) | No | Most general purpose use |\n",
    "| MergeSort | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n) | Yes | Guaranteed performance, stable sort |\n",
    "\n",
    "*With optimization (early exit when no swaps)\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Challenge Problems\n",
    "\n",
    "Try implementing these to deepen your understanding:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Optimized Bubble Sort** - Add early exit when already sorted\n",
    "2. **Hybrid Sort** - Use Insertion Sort for small arrays, QuickSort for large ones\n",
    "3. **Sort Objects** - Sort by multiple fields (age, then name)\n",
    "4. **Three-Way Partitioning** - Better QuickSort with many duplicates\n",
    "5. **Count Inversions** - Count pairs (i,j) where i < j but arr[i] > arr[j]\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 💡 Learning Tips\n",
    "\n",
    "✓ **Elementary sorts (Bubble, Selection, Insertion)** = O(n²), good for small data  \n",
    "✓ **Advanced sorts (Quick, Merge)** = O(n log n), necessary for large data  \n",
    "✓ **Stability** = preserves order of equal elements (matters when sorting objects)  \n",
    "✓ **In-place** = doesn't need extra memory (QuickSort does, MergeSort doesn't)  \n",
    "✓ **C#'s Sort()** uses Introsort (hybrid: QuickSort + HeapSort + InsertionSort)  \n",
    "✓ Implement each one, then experiment by modifying the code  \n",
